@page "/"

<AdminOnly>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h4" GutterBottom="true">IT Helpdesk &amp; Assets</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Color="Color.Secondary" Class="mb-4">
        Four independently deployable services, one REST gateway, one event pipeline.
    </MudText>

    <MudPaper Class="pa-4 mb-4">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" GutterBottom="true">What to look at</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            Open <strong>Tickets</strong>, create one, then watch the overview panel fill in. The
            assignment and the SLA clock arrive from two different services that never speak to
            each other &mdash; both are reacting to the same <code>Ticket.Created</code> event,
            independently.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Color="Color.Secondary">
            Everything on this page came over HTTP/JSON from the gateway. This application has no
            gRPC client, no <code>.proto</code> file, and no idea that four backends exist.
        </MudText>
    </MudPaper>

    <MudPaper Class="pa-4">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" GutterBottom="true">Proving it the hard way</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
            Stop <code>assignment-api</code> and create another ticket. The SLA clock still starts.
            Bring it back up and it will pick up the queued event on its own. That is the difference
            between a fan-out and a chain.
        </MudText>
    </MudPaper>
</AdminOnly>
